MORE(1)                        P32W Unix                         MORE(1)

NAME
       more - page through text

SYNOPSIS
       more [file ...]

DESCRIPTION
       more copies each file in turn, or the standard input if there
       are none, to the standard output, stopping after every
       screenful (one line less than the screen has: 23 lines on a
       24-line screen) with the prompt --More--. A line longer than
       the screen is wide counts as the screen rows it wraps onto.
       more then waits for one key, read from the terminal /dev/tty
       (so more works at the end of a pipe), with echo off and
       without waiting for return:

       space  show the next screenful
       return show one more line
       q      quit
              any other key shows the next screenful, like space.

       Files follow one another with no header between them; the
       count of lines starts again with each file. A file that cannot
       be opened is reported and skipped.

       When the standard output is not a terminal, or there is no
       terminal to read keys from (/dev/tty cannot be opened), more
       does not page: it copies the files like cat(1), with no
       prompts.

       man(1) shows manual pages through more.

EXAMPLES
       more /etc/passwd
       ls -l /bin | more
       grep -n main *.c | more

ENVIRONMENT
       LINES       the screen's height in lines (default 24)
       COLUMNS     its width in characters (default 80)

FILES
       /dev/tty    the terminal keys are read from

EXIT STATUS
       0; 1 if a file could not be opened.

SEE ALSO
       man(1), cat(1), head(1), tail(1), stty(1)

BUGS
       The screen size is not asked of the terminal: set LINES and
       COLUMNS (and export them) if it is not 24 by 80. Wrapping is
       worked out assuming tab stops every 8 columns and that all
       other characters are one column wide.

       There is no going back, no searching, no -n or + options and
       no file name or percentage in the prompt. - does not mean the
       standard input.
